[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that lets you move the map smoothly to any latitude/longitude (or to a bounding box) when the user clicks a button.  
It uses the native `map.flyTo()` / `map.easeTo()` animation helpers that MapLibre GL JS provides, and it follows the cartographic best-practice notes from the **maplibre-cartography** skill (contrast-friendly halos, layer ordering, etc.) so the transition won’t accidentally hide labels or break accessibility.

---

## 1. Basic HTML & CSS

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre fly-to demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .control {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      padding: 6px 10px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: sans-serif;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div class="control" id="flyBtn">Fly to Paris</div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="fly-to.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*The button (`#flyBtn`) is a simple, high-contrast control that meets WCAG 2.1 AA (white text on a semi-transparent white background). Feel free to replace it with a `<button>` element if you need native focus/keyboard support.*

---

## 2. Initialise the map (fly-to.js)

```js
// ==== 1️⃣  Create the map ==================================================
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any vector style you like
  center: [-0.1276, 51.5074],           // London (lon, lat)
  zoom: 10,
  pitch: 0,
  bearing: 0,
  // If you are using satellite imagery, remember the halo recommendations:
  // text-color: "#ffffff", text-halo-color: "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)"
});

// ==== 2️⃣  Wait for the style to load before adding UI logic ==============
map.on('load', () => {
  // Optional: add a marker at the target location so the user sees *what* they’re flying to
  const target = new maplibregl.Marker({color: '#ff6600'})
    .setLngLat([2.3522, 48.8566]) // Paris
    .addTo(map);

  // ==== 3️⃣  Hook the button =================================================
  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => flyToParis());
});
```

---

## 3. The smooth fly-to function

```js
/**
 * Fly the camera to Paris with a pleasant easing curve.
 *
 * The options below are the most common “cartographic” knobs:
 *   • `center` – where the camera points (lon, lat)
 *   • `zoom`   – how close we get (10-12 is city-scale)
 *   • `bearing`/`pitch` – optional rotation / 3-D tilt
 *   • `duration` – total animation time in ms (default 1000)
 *   • `curve` – how “tight” the flight path feels (default 1.42)
 *   • `essential` – true tells the browser to honour the animation even
 *                   when the user has “reduced-motion” preferences disabled.
 */
function flyToParis() {
  map.flyTo({
    // 1️⃣ Target location (lon, lat)
    center: [2.3522, 48.8566],

    // 2️⃣ Zoom level – 12 shows streets, 14+ shows building footprints
    zoom: 12,

    // 3️⃣ Optional orientation – a slight north-east bearing makes the view feel dynamic
    bearing: 30,          // degrees clockwise from north
    pitch: 45,            // tilt the camera for a 3-D feel (0-60° is safe)

    // 4️⃣ Animation timing
    duration: 2000,       // 2 seconds – long enough to read the motion
    curve: 1.5,           // a smoother, slightly slower start
    easing: t => t,       // linear easing (feel free to use d3-ease or custom)

    // 5️⃣ Accessibility flag – if the user prefers reduced motion, the map will jump instantly
    essential: true
  });
}
```

### Why `flyTo` (instead of `easeTo`)?

| Feature | `flyTo` | `easeTo` |
|---------|---------|----------|
| **Arc-like flight** (camera lifts, then drops) | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Control over “curve”** (how high the camera lifts) | ✅ | ❌ |
| **Same API for bearing/pitch** | ✅ | ✅ |
| **Simpler for “go to a point”** | ✅ | ✅ (but you lose the natural “fly-over” feel) |

If you only need a straight-line pan (no lift), replace `flyTo` with `easeTo` and drop the `curve` option.

---

## 4. Keeping basemap labels readable during the transition

When you animate over a **satellite / aerial imagery** basemap, the label halo must stay visible the whole time. The style you load should already have the recommended halo:

```json
{
  "type": "symbol",
  "layout": { "text-field": "{name}" },
  "paint": {
    "text-color": "#ffffff",
    "text-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
    "text-halo-width": 1.2
  }
}
```

If you are switching between a light vector basemap and an imagery basemap at runtime, you can update the halo on the fly:

```js
function setImageryLabelStyle() {
  const labelLayers = map.getStyle().layers.filter(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  labelLayers.forEach(l => {
    map.setPaintProperty(l.id, 'text-color', '#ffffff');
    map.setPaintProperty(l.id, 'text-halo-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0.75)');
    map.setPaintProperty(l.id, 'text-halo-width', 1.2);
  });
}
```

Call `setImageryLabelStyle()` right after you swap the raster source, and the labels will stay legible throughout the flight.

---

## 5. Optional: Fly to a **bounding box** instead of a single point

If you want the camera to zoom so that a whole area (e.g., a city polygon) fits, use `fitBounds`:

```js
function flyToBounds() {
  const bounds = [
    [2.2241, 48.8156], // southwest (lon, lat)
    [2.4699, 48.9022]  // northeast
  ];
  map.fitBounds(bounds, {
    padding: {top: 40, bottom: 40, left: 40, right: 40},
    duration: 1500,
    curve: 1.4,
    essential: true
  });
}
```

`fitBounds` internally calls `flyTo` with a calculated center/zoom, so all the same halo/contrast rules apply.

